"executive_summary": "This work investigates highly concentrated aqueous binary electrolytes based on potassium acetate (CH3COOK) combined with varying amounts of sodium acetate (CH3COONa), aiming to develop safe, low-cost, and environmentally friendly electrolytes for aqueous sodium-ion batteries. The motivation stems from the limitations of conventional lithium-ion batteries, which rely on flammable, toxic, and expensive organic electrolytes, and from the narrow thermodynamic electrochemical stability window of water (1.23 V), which can only be kinetically extended to about 1.5 V in dilute solutions. Super-concentrated 'water-in-salt' electrolytes offer a pathway to widen this window by reducing the activity of water molecules through strong ion hydration. While fluorinated salts such as LiTFSI and NaFSI have been widely used, they suffer from toxicity, cost, and availability issues. In contrast, acetate salts like CH3COOK and CH3COONa are cost-effective, highly soluble, and widely available, making them attractive alternatives. The authors prepared four binary electrolytes with a constant CH3COOK concentration of 20 mol kg⁻¹ and varying CH3COONa concentrations of 1, 2.5, 5, and 7 mol kg⁻¹, along with a 1 M NaAc reference solution. They comprehensively characterized these electrolytes using differential scanning calorimetry (DSC), rheology, conductivity measurements, and electrochemical techniques. The results show that increasing salt concentration shifts the glass transition temperature to higher values, indicating stronger ion-water interactions and a transition from salt-in-water to water-in-salt behavior. The most concentrated solution, 7N20K (7 mol kg⁻¹ NaAc + 20 mol kg⁻¹ KAc), exhibited the best compromise between transport properties and electrochemical stability, with a conductivity of 21.2 mS cm⁻¹ at 25°C and an electrochemical stability window up to 3 V under ideal conditions. As a proof of concept for sodium-ion batteries, a carbon-coated LiTi2(PO4)3 (NASICON) electrode demonstrated reversible Na⁺ insertion and de-insertion at about -0.7 V vs. SHE, with a first-cycle anodic capacity of 85 mAh g⁻¹, an average charge efficiency of 96% at low current, and 90% capacity retention after 60 cycles. The low charge transfer activation energy of 0.12 eV highlights the excellent interfacial kinetics. These findings demonstrate the potential of highly concentrated aqueous acetate electrolytes for developing safe and sustainable alkaline-ion rechargeable batteries.",
